import { TooltipProvider } from './TooltipContext';
import Button from './Button';
import Tooltip from './Tooltip';

const App = () => {
  return (
    <TooltipProvider>
      <Button tooltip="This is a tooltip">Click me</Button>
      <Button tooltip="This is another tooltip">Click me again</Button>
    </TooltipProvider>
  );
};

export default App;
```

In this example, we create a `TooltipContext` using the `createContext` function from React. We then create a `TooltipProvider` component that wraps the app and provides the `openTooltip`, `closeTooltip`, and `tooltip` state to its children.

The `Tooltip` component uses the `useContext` hook to access the `closeTooltip` function and the `tooltip` state from the context. If the `tooltip` state is truthy, it renders the tooltip with a close button.

The `Button` component uses the `useContext` hook to access the `openTooltip` function from the context. When the button is clicked, it opens the tooltip with the specified text.

Finally, in the `App` component, we wrap the app with the `TooltipProvider` and pass the `tooltip` text to the `Button` components. When a button is clicked, it opens the corresponding tooltip. When the close button is clicked, it closes the tooltip. If another button is clicked, it closes the previously opened tooltip and opens the new one. This way, we can have multiple tooltips open at the same time, and they will close each other when a new one is opened.

